      a: "Three things: its own state changed via a `useState`/`useReducer` setter with a value that isn't `Object.is`-equal to the current one; its parent re-rendered, which by default re-renders all children whether or not their props changed; or a context it consumes got a new value. A subscribed external store updating also counts. Note what is NOT on the list: mutating an object in place, and changing `ref.current` — neither notifies React. The critical distinction is that re-rendering means React calls your function again and diffs the result; it does not mean the DOM was touched. If the output is identical, the diff finds nothing and no DOM operation happens, which is why 'extra renders' are usually cheap and rarely worth optimising blindly.",
