      q: "What is a stale closure in React, and how do you avoid it?",
      a: "Every render creates fresh functions that close over that render's props and state. If a function outlives the render that made it — inside a `setInterval`, an event listener, or an effect with an incomplete dependency array — it keeps reading the old values. The classic case is `useEffect(() => { const id = setInterval(() => setCount(count + 1), 1000); return () => clearInterval(id); }, [])`: the interval captures `count` as 0 forever, so the counter sticks at 1. Three fixes, in order of preference: use the functional updater `setCount(c => c + 1)` so you never read the captured value; put the value in the dependency array and let the effect re-subscribe; or, when re-subscribing is too expensive, keep the latest value in a ref that the callback reads at call time.",
